{% extends layout %}

{% block subject %}
Your new SRCF administrative account
{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
Welcome to the SRCF system administration team -- thank you for joining us!

We've created a new account for you to perform all administrative tasks:

    Username: {{ username }}
    Password: {{ password }}

Security of this account is very important!  Please immediately change the
password to something unguessable, and make it different from your regular
SRCF account password.

Your new administrative account is a member of the `sysadmins` user group,
and as a consequence has full root access via the `sudo` command to most of
our servers.

The policy regarding use of this access is:

  * Do not log in as `root` directly –- this means no use of `su`,
    `sudo -i`, `sudo -s` and so on.

    Execute all commands via `sudo` from your normal account.  This
    improves logging and accountability.

  * Keep your normal and sysadmin accounts as separate as possible.

    Do not `su` from one to the other, or give one a private key
    which will provide access to the other.  If your website is
    compromised, this should not mean pip is rooted.

More information on being an administrator for the SRCF can be found in the
executive wiki, which you should have been granted access to:

    https://wiki.executive.srcf.net

If you have any questions, however silly, please ask –- it will help our
woefully neglected documentation process!  :)

Finally, and most importantly, you should be on #sysadmin on the SRCF IRC
network, irc.srcf.net.  It's recommended that you use a bouncer or persistent
client (e.g. running irssi inside screen/tmux on pip or another shell server)
so that you don't miss any messages.

With great power comes great responsibility.
{% endblock %}
